@Steve L

Exactly.  We morning solvers see the chatter from folks who solved 12 hours prior to our morning coffee.  It's a whole different world.  But like many online conversation groups, there's a rush to post the first comment.  That (and a super-fast solve time) seems to be valued, like a second gold star.

To each their own, but I'd rather solve a Friday on a Friday and take a good, long, leisurely time of it.  And when I have to resort to a Google lookup, if the found answer is an interesting topic, I'll read the Wikipedia page while the timer just keeps on ticking.  Making the solve experience into a hot dog eating contest is just foreign to me.

@Alex

As I predicted, the rating wasn't going to change much.  At most, it shifts one notch.  Never to the other end of the spectrum.

And even though it has its faults, xwstats.com is the only quantifiable database we have. I'm sure you're right that it skews towards crossword veterans, but is that a bad thing?  It shows relative difficulty among a group that supposedly knows what it's doing. Otherwise, you get wild swings among less experienced solvers, who often get messed up because they miss a few key things here and there.

@Curtis

As Pezhead pointed out, 44% solved faster than average, and 23% solved more than 20% faster than average.

The difficulty designation is itself an average.  It is to be expected that there will be outliers.

