I guess saying “trust me” only goes so far in an anonymous forum, but that said…trust me: I finished today’s puzzle without any manner of help, and in about a third of the time it takes me to solve most Fridays from 10+ years ago.

@Millie

I don’t know how long you’ve been doing the NYT puzzle, or what amount of pondering you consider fun, but it probably depends on the mix of newer vs. long time NYT xword solvers. 

Resisting the urge to check Wordplay or otherwise looking things up, even when you think you’re totally stuck, eventually leads to greater and greater success. Setting the puzzle aside and coming back to it can change your perspective on a clue. Eventually, you see clues from multiple angles immediately, stretch your brain laterally and, of course, recognize tricks from the past. If you rush too quickly to look things up you deny yourself that longer term gain. The Google world has produced many benefits but has also encouraged us to just click before stretching our brains as much as we used to. Stubbornness and patience, here, work wonders. On the other hand, lookups, especially after the fact, can lead to great rabbit holes. But, whatever makes this fun for you, go for it.

